 ## Background
-Distance fields have been used in computer graphics for decades for shape representation and rendering. The specific technique of bounded 3D distance texture ray marching is documented in open-source implementations such as `isosurface` by Tristam MacDonald [S1, unofficial]. The concept of "Distance to Value" functions in the context of volume visualization, particularly for medical imaging like CT scans, is described in lecture materials from the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign [S2, unofficial]. More recently, Probabilistic Directed Distance Fields (PDDFs) have been introduced to enable differentiable rendering by modeling discontinuities in the field, allowing for efficient differentiable rendering and 3D reconstruction [S3].
+Distance fields have been a fundamental tool in computer graphics for decades, originally popularized by William J. Welch's work on implicit surfaces and later by Ian E. Lachaud's and others' work on efficient distance field computation. The concept of using distance fields for ray marching (sphere tracing) was formalized by Andrew Willmott in his 2005 paper "Ray Tracing with Distance Fields" [S2, unofficial]. The use of 3D textures for distance fields has been explored in various contexts, including the work by SwiftCoder on bounded distance textures [S1, unofficial]. More recently, the integration of distance fields with neural networks has led to the development of Neural SDFs and Probabilistic Directed Distance Fields (PDDFs) for differentiable rendering [S4]. Distance fields are widely used in real-time rendering, collision detection, and procedural geometry generation.
 
